# ansible-lint, on top of the exact control node the deploy uses. Compiled to
# requirements-lint.txt with hashes:
#
#   pip-compile --allow-unsafe --generate-hashes --strip-extras \
#     --output-file=ansible/requirements-lint.txt ansible/requirements-lint.in
#
# The -r line is the whole point. ansible-core stays pinned once, in
# requirements-ansible.in, and the linter inherits that pin. Pinning it a
# second time here would let CI lint against one Ansible and deploy with
# another — the same drift the control-node pin exists to prevent, moved one
# file to the left.
#
# Recompile both files in the same commit. requirements-lint.in includes
# requirements-ansible.in, so compiling one alone lets the two locks resolve at
# different times — the failure gunicorn already demonstrated once.
-r requirements-ansible.in

# Pinned like hadolint, for the same reason: a linter that gains a rule
# overnight fails a build nobody touched. Bump it deliberately, in its own
# commit, so a new finding is attributable.
ansible-lint==26.6.0
